hi there steve kaufman here this is the  third  of these 12 videos  that will accompany you through these  three months where you are working to  make a breakthrough in your language  learning  i want to focus on different sort of  activities different aspects of  the things that you can do to improve  during these three months these 90 days  and today i want to talk about listening  the importance of listening how to make  listening more  effective and  why it's so important  so  as you know  modern language acquisition  research indicates that input input of  comprehensible and compelling material  is  vital to our  acquisition of the language it's not a  matter of explaining the language or  drilling you in the language it's a  matter of you exposing yourself to a lot  of compelling content obviously this can  be done by reading but  listening has a lot of advantages  listening is easy to arrange you can do  it anywhere you can do it five minutes  at a time 10 minutes at a time  we have this lovely uh print behind me  one of my favorite prints of southern  france done by van gogh but this also  happens to be our kitchen here in palm  springs and it's a major listening area  for me because i'm usually the one who  cleans up  i also use the swifter to clean the  floor  any activity that i do around the house  i can do while listening  so listening is very convenient in that  regard  it's easy too it's sometimes you fade  out sometimes you focus better  um  it's easy to do it can be a little more  challenging to read and of course it  takes a lot more work to arrange to find  someone who can speak to you in the  language that you're learning so  listening is the easiest thing to  arrange  and it's very effective i find that that  reading helps with vocabulary  acquisition but listening prepares you  for speaking especially if you listen  more than once to the same content at  least at the beginning of your journey  in a new language later on as you  progress you don't need to listen more  than once when you're at a sort of a  high intermediate level but when you're  at the beginner level i find very it  very useful to listen more than once to  the same content and when i say more  than once i'm talking 30 40 50 times  uh in my recent efforts at learning  arabic and farsi and even greek before  then where we have these mini stories  which use the most frequently used verbs  in the language and they repeat within  the same story three four five times the  same structures the same verbs maybe  slightly different forms of the verb or  different tenses  and so this exposure repetitive exposure  and then listening to it over and over  and over again noticing different things  each time prepares your brain makes the  brain more familiar with the language  and so then when you go to speak you  find that you're able to speak much  earlier at least in my own case much  earlier than was the case before so  reading excuse me listening is easy to  arrange you can do it whenever you want  and it's tremendously powerful for  improving your speaking and also your  pronunciation now  one should  equip oneself for effective listening so  first of all if you are listening at  link if you're listening to a lesson  make sure that you download that to your  playlist because then you'll always have  it with you and it goes across different  devices so if i save  a lesson the audio to a playlist on my  computer it is automatically available  for me on my ipod nano  it's available for me on my  ipad the larger ipad which i use mostly  for reading this i use if i go to the  gym  it's available for me on my iphone which  is filming this video  and so not only is it readily available  but  it tracks how much listening you do so  you develop meaningful statistics on how  much you've been listening across the  various months that you've been studying  the language and you can look at these  in your profile at link per language or  if it's one language when did you start  when are you doing more listening less  listening and all of that is very  helpful uh so make sure you use the  playlist one other thing about  listening  personally i'm a great fan of these  little guys the  uh airpods now you might think i'm a  walking promotion for apple but it is  these are tremendously convenient but  you can also use these conventional  um  earpods but the thing is get something  that is is functional that you like that  suits your budget so that you can be  equipped to listen  and to track your listening  on  online  so  that's what i wanted to emphasize that  that language learning is not  uh  a matter of of getting a grammar book  and devouring this grammar book in fact  if anything i find after a while after  you've heard certain structures in the  language you become curious and you may  look up a grammar point but the main  thing is to do a lot of listening and  reading  and the listening and maybe i should say  some people say they have trouble  focusing when listening get content that  you're interested in initially  in the early stages you have to listen  through content that may not be that  interesting in order to get a toehold in  the language but eventually there's on  the internet there's an unlimited supply  of podcasts and interesting content  which i have used for all the various  languages i've learned over the last 10  years  there has never been a better  age in which to be learning languages  content abounds convenient tools  abound  so go to it and make sure you listen as  much as you can in order to improve in  your language so thank you for listening  bye for now
 

